            "id": "node.topic.surgical-infection-antibiotics.old-drain-colonization.v2",
            "questionVariantId": "question.topic.surgical-infection-antibiotics.old-drain-colonization.v2",
            "primaryConceptId": "concept.surgical-infection-antibiotics.old-drain-culture-interpretation.v1",
            "currentUpdate": "Maya Grant completed four full days of active antibacterial treatment after drainage of her postoperative collection and has been off treatment since then. At clinic she is afebrile, eating and has less pain; her leukocyte count remains above the laboratory reference range but continues to fall. The completion study documented an emptied cavity without an ongoing leak. The retained catheter was left for output/removal follow-up. A specimen later collected from its disinfected sampling port, after the catheter had remained in place throughout recovery, reports Pseudomonas susceptible to cefepime and meropenem. No new collection was accessed and no blood cultures were obtained. Clinical and drain follow-up continue with every plan.",
            "stem": "Which antibacterial plan is most appropriate for Maya Grant now?",
            "showServicePreviews": false,
            "answerChoices": [
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.surgical-infection-antibiotics.old-drain-colonization.no-restart.v2",
                "label": "Do not restart antibacterial therapy",
                "isCorrect": true,
                "rationale": "A current port isolate from an indwelling system does not establish a new source infection. Her improving course after adequate control and completed treatment does not justify restarting solely for the report or a declining mildly abnormal inflammatory marker.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.surgical-infection-antibiotics.old-drain-colonization.cefepime.v2",
                "label": "Restart IV cefepime",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "Cefepime is active against the isolate, but the report's device provenance and current recovery do not establish an indication to treat it.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.surgical-infection-antibiotics.old-drain-colonization.meropenem.v2",
                "label": "Restart IV meropenem",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "Meropenem is active, but greater spectrum does not overcome the missing evidence of recurrent infection and would treat an indwelling-system result.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              }
            ],
            "shuffleAnswers": true,
            "explanation": "Collection technique and source validity are separate. Disinfecting the port does not make material from a longstanding catheter equivalent to direct access to a new cavity. Interpret that report with Maya's clinical recovery, documented control and completed course: do not restart for susceptibility results or a falling leukocyte count in isolation. New illness would require reassessment rather than automatic dismissal of future cultures.",
