            "primaryConceptId": "concept.pancreas-acute-pancreatitis.volume-overload-fluid-reassessment.v1",
            "currentUpdate": "During the next hospital interval the infusion continued and cumulative balance rose markedly. {patientName} developed new oxygen need, bibasal crackles and diffuse lung ultrasound B-lines. Blood pressure was 126/76 mmHg, urine output had recovered and BUN was falling. Bedside assessment found a plethoric vena cava.",
            "stem": "Which immediate fluid adjustment should the clinic review recommend at {patientName}'s new assessment?",
            "answerChoices": [
              {
                "id": "choice.pancreas-acute-pancreatitis.fluid-overload.reduce",
                "label": "Reduce or stop the crystalloid infusion and reassess",
                "isCorrect": true,
                "rationale": "New congestion with restored perfusion calls for reversing continued fluid loading and reassessing support.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.pancreas-acute-pancreatitis.fluid-overload.bolus",
                "label": "Give another rapid crystalloid bolus and reassess",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "The current findings argue against additional empiric volume.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.pancreas-acute-pancreatitis.fluid-overload.continue",
                "label": "Continue the current crystalloid infusion and reassess",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "Continuing the same exposure ignores new pulmonary and venous congestion.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              }
            ],
            "shuffleAnswers": true,
            "explanation": "The appropriate fluid plan changes with the patient. The combined congestion findings and restored perfusion support reducing or stopping ongoing fluid loading, with assessment of respiratory and hemodynamic support. A falling BUN is not permission to keep infusing despite overload.",
