        "learningSummary": "Large citrate exposure can lower ionized calcium, impairing coagulation and cardiovascular function. The measured deficit requires IV calcium; with peripheral access, calcium gluconate is the better fit among these prescriptions. Calcium chloride is more irritating and is generally reserved for central administration. Continue ECG, access-site and ionized-calcium monitoring; these formulations are not dose-for-dose interchangeable."
