            "primaryConceptId": "concept.preoperative-nutrition.oral-enteral-first-line",
            "currentUpdate": "The dietitian introduced fortified meals and oral supplements. Over repeated reviews, fatigue ends meals after a few mouthfuls, weight continues to fall and consumed supplements remain mostly unfinished. Swallow assessment shows no aspiration. A contrast examination obtained for abdominal symptoms shows passage through stomach and small bowel without hold-up.",
            "stem": "Which nutrition route should {patientName} use next after the documented oral treatment trial?",
            "answerChoices": [
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.nutrition-support-metabolism.oral-escalation.gastric.v1",
                "label": "Nasogastric tube nutrition",
                "isCorrect": true,
                "rationale": "The oral plan has actually failed while swallowing and gastrointestinal transit do not show a barrier to gastric tube delivery.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.nutrition-support-metabolism.oral-escalation.oral.v1",
                "label": "Fortified meals with additional oral supplements",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "The implemented strategy remains unable to supply intake because the patient cannot finish it; another oral prescription does not establish delivery.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.nutrition-support-metabolism.oral-escalation.pn.v1",
                "label": "Central parenteral nutrition",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "Neither oral failure nor low albumin establishes that enteral delivery is infeasible. Tube nutrition is the next route to assess.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              }
            ],
            "shuffleAnswers": true,
            "explanation": "Interpret the failed trial as a problem delivering enough by mouth, then select a usable enteral route. Oral inadequacy and intestinal failure are different boundaries.",
